WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.450
Welcome to Module One: LLM Architecture.

2
00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.951
You'll open up a language model and build a small one yourself.

3
00:00:07.751 --> 00:00:11.797
Meet Priya, asked to fine-tune a small open model for her support desk.

4
00:00:12.127 --> 00:00:15.576
She opens its config file and stares at unfamiliar names.

5
00:00:16.376 --> 00:00:21.766
RoPE theta, KV heads, intermediate size: each one changes memory, speed and cost.

6
00:00:22.096 --> 00:00:24.841
Without them, she can't tell if it fits her GPU.

7
00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:27.660
This module gives her, and you, the map.

8
00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:31.759
We build up from tensors until that config reads like plain English.

9
00:00:32.559 --> 00:00:36.370
Every LLM, toy or frontier, is trained by the same loop.

10
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.298
Get a batch, compute the loss, backpropagate, and update the weights.

11
00:00:41.098 --> 00:00:45.592
Lesson one teaches the PyTorch behind it: tensor shapes and autograd.

12
00:00:45.922 --> 00:00:49.157
You'll train a tiny bigram model on Shakespeare in under a minute.

13
00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:51.785
Lesson two adds self-attention.

14
00:00:52.115 --> 00:00:56.737
Each token's query meets earlier tokens' keys and gathers a mix of their values.

15
00:00:57.537 --> 00:01:01.732
A causal mask hides the future, and several heads look for different patterns.

16
00:01:02.062 --> 00:01:06.364
Add an MLP and residual connections, and you have a transformer block.

17
00:01:07.164 --> 00:01:11.466
In the lab, you stack those blocks into a three-million-parameter GPT.

18
00:01:11.796 --> 00:01:15.138
You'll watch it go from random characters to passable Shakespeare.

19
00:01:15.938 --> 00:01:18.448
You'll steer it with temperature and top-k sampling.

20
00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.013
And you'll predict its size with the 12·L·d² rule.

21
00:01:22.813 --> 00:01:25.771
Lesson four brings that 2019 design up to date.

22
00:01:26.101 --> 00:01:31.193
Modern models add rotary positions, RMSNorm and SwiGLU layers.

23
00:01:31.993 --> 00:01:36.081
Grouped-query attention lets many query heads share a few key-value heads.

24
00:01:36.411 --> 00:01:41.183
That shrinks the KV cache, which decides how many users a GPU can serve.

25
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:45.965
Mixture-of-experts models send each token to just a few experts.

26
00:01:46.295 --> 00:01:53.007
Mixtral 8×7B holds 47 billion parameters, but each token uses about 13 billion.

27
00:01:53.807 --> 00:01:56.680
Scaling laws tell you how to spend a compute budget.

28
00:01:57.010 --> 00:02:00.885
Chinchilla's rule of thumb: about 20 training tokens per parameter.

29
00:02:01.685 --> 00:02:06.201
Back to Priya: 24 layers, 14 query heads sharing 2 KV heads.

30
00:02:06.531 --> 00:02:09.297
Now she can estimate memory and explain the model.

31
00:02:10.097 --> 00:02:14.420
By the end, you'll have trained your own GPT and can read any model config.

32
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.095
Let's begin with lesson one: tensors, autograd and your first training loop.
